{
  "header": [
    "PEERS.diag — the peer set rs-conformance declares. ADR-0003 §7: membership is a RULE, not a",
    "hand-typed list, and a verdict records a peer's IDENTITY rather than its name.",
    "",
    "⛔ THE NESTING CONDITION IS UNRESOLVED AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT GAMED HERE. ADR-0003 §7.3",
    "refuses two suites whose peer sets are identical or nested. Suite 1 declares the whole",
    "keystone roster, so EVERY set this suite could declare over keystone peers is nested inside",
    "it, by arithmetic rather than by choice. The brief's instruction is explicit — raise it, do",
    "not quietly shrink either side to make a gate go green — so the set below is chosen on its",
    "own argument and the condition is declared as an open ask, not engineered away.",
    "",
    "⚠⚠ AND A CONTRADICTION IN THE BRIEF, REPORTED RATHER THAN RESOLVED BY GUESSING. The brief's",
    "§6 tells this session to read a note called `suite_2_note`, which lives inside suite 1's own",
    "peer-set declaration. The brief's §2 forbids opening ANY FILE of suite 1, because reading it",
    "makes this suite a copy of it. The peer-binding tool's diversity lint points a reader at the",
    "same note. THE NOTE WAS NOT READ, and the two instructions cannot both be followed.",
    "Whatever argument it makes about forced nesting is therefore absent from this file, and the",
    "rationale below was reached independently from ADR-0003 §7.3 and the roster alone. If that",
    "note already answers this question, the answer was unreachable from inside the authoring",
    "seat — which is itself the finding, and is reported."
  ],

  "suite": "rs-conformance",

  "rationale": [
    "THE SET IS CHOSEN TO BE DIFFERENT IN KIND, NOT MERELY SMALLER.",
    "",
    "Three instruments now exist and all three are written in imperative, C-descended languages:",
    "the reference oracle in Go, suite 1 in Python, this suite in Rust. AGENTS.md's own founding",
    "argument is that a shared language is shared idioms. That argument does not stop at the",
    "instrument — it applies to the PEER as well. A peer written in the same family as the",
    "instrument scoring it is the peer where a shared-idiom defect is LEAST visible, because both",
    "sides make the same assumption and agree for a reason that has nothing to do with the",
    "protocol. Suite 1's Go-era ancestor is the worked example: it refused every CBOR float and",
    "skipped 20 corpus vectors for reasons that were about Go (AP-10).",
    "",
    "So this set privileges peers whose language paradigm is FURTHEST from all three instrument",
    "languages: lazy-functional, ML-family, Lisp, logic, object-message, declarative-query,",
    "dataflow, assembly, and a legacy business language. A CBOR or hashing assumption that three",
    "C-descended instruments share is most likely to be contradicted by a peer whose language",
    "offers it no idiom to borrow.",
    "",
    "⛔ AND GO, PYTHON AND RUST PEERS ARE EXCLUDED ON PURPOSE (see `exclusions`). Not because they",
    "are bad peers — they are the best-maintained ones. Because THIS suite scoring THOSE peers is",
    "the measurement least able to detect the failure this suite exists to detect.",
    "",
    "⚠ WHAT THIS SET IS NOT: it is not a claim of coverage, and it is not the full matrix. The",
    "target is every peer against every other; where the full matrix is too expensive the pairs",
    "actually run are DECLARED. This is that declaration, and it is a sample chosen by an argument",
    "rather than by convenience."
  ],

  "sources": [
    {
      "source": "keystone",
      "peers": [
        "haskell",
        "lean",
        "ocaml",
        "common-lisp",
        "prolog",
        "smalltalk",
        "sql",
        "cobol",
        "asm-x86_64",
        "forth"
      ],
      "note": [
        "Ten of the roster's forty-six. Paradigm coverage, one per family where the roster allows:",
        "lazy-functional (haskell) · dependent-typed (lean) · ML (ocaml) · Lisp (common-lisp) ·",
        "logic (prolog) · object-message (smalltalk) · declarative-query (sql) · legacy-business",
        "(cobol) · assembly (asm-x86_64) · concatenative-stack (forth).",
        "",
        "⚠ IDENTITY, NOT NAME. Each of these resolves through keystone's own roster and report at",
        "the moment of use; nothing about a peer is stored in this file. 3 of 46 peers carry a",
        "contract certification, so most rows will read `contract_certified: absent`, and that is",
        "the honest value rather than an omission."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "core-go",
      "peers": [
        "core-go"
      ],
      "note": [
        "The reference implementation, included DESPITE the language-distance rule above and for",
        "the opposite reason: it is the peer whose behaviour has most shaped the cohort, so a",
        "disagreement between this suite and the oracle ON THIS PEER is the single most",
        "informative row either instrument can produce. Its identity is recorded as a PIN",
        "(`peer_tree` / `image_id`) with `certified_by_contract: false` — a pin says WHICH BYTES,",
        "a certification says they were CHECKED, and conflating the two is not available here."
      ]
    }
  ],

  "exclusions": [
    {
      "peers": [
        "go",
        "python",
        "rust"
      ],
      "reason": [
        "DELIBERATE, ARGUED, AND NOT A CONVENIENCE. These three are the languages of the three",
        "existing instruments (oracle: Go · suite 1: Python · this suite: Rust). A shared-idiom",
        "defect between an instrument and a peer of the same language is invisible by",
        "construction: both sides inherit the same codec habit, the same integer-width",
        "assumption, the same float posture, and they agree. Excluding them makes this set's",
        "agreements mean more, not less.",
        "",
        "⚠ THIS IS AN EXCLUSION FROM THIS SUITE'S DECLARED SET, NOT A CLAIM THEY ARE UNIMPORTANT.",
        "Suite 1 declares the whole roster and reaches all three. Joint coverage is the point:",
        "`suite A reaches X, suite B reaches Y, together Z`. A peer nobody reaches would be a",
        "gap; a peer one suite reaches is coverage."
      ]
    }
  ],

  "open_asks": [
    {
      "id": "RSC-PEERS-1",
      "ask": "Does ADR-0003 §7.3's nested-set refusal bind when the outer set is the WHOLE roster — i.e. when nesting is forced by arithmetic and no choice of subset can avoid it?",
      "owner": "entity-system-architecture, with entity-system-conformance",
      "state": "OPEN. Not routed by this session; raised here so the next session does not re-derive it. ⭐ `lint-peer-diversity` DOES fire now — the brief recorded that it could not, and declaring this set made it. It reports clause 3 against the `core-go` source.",
      "not_done": "This suite did NOT shrink its set, and did NOT propose shrinking suite 1's, to make the condition go away. Either would answer a design question with a gate edit."
    },
    {
      "id": "RSC-PEERS-3",
      "ask": "ADR-0003 §7.3 clause 3 refuses two suites declaring identical peer sets for one source. Is it satisfiable AT ALL for a source that contains exactly one peer?",
      "owner": "entity-system-architecture, with entity-system-conformance",
      "state": [
        "OPEN, and found by declaring this set rather than by review. The `core-go` source has ONE",
        "member. Two suites that both test it necessarily declare identical sets for it, so clause 3",
        "fires; the only way to silence it is for one suite to stop testing the reference",
        "implementation — which is the peer whose behaviour has most shaped the cohort and therefore",
        "the one a second opinion is most valuable about.",
        "",
        "⛔ The clause is well-aimed at a multi-member source, where an identical set really is one",
        "instrument wearing two names. On a singleton it inverts: it penalises exactly the overlap",
        "that makes a cross-instrument comparison possible. A disagreement between two suites is",
        "only observable ON A PEER BOTH RAN.",
        "",
        "⇒ Suggested shape of an answer, not adopted here: clause 3 keys on sources with more than",
        "one member, or the finding is downgraded to a note for singleton sources."
      ],
      "not_done": "core-go was NOT dropped from this declaration to make `make lint` green. It was run against, and it produced this session's most informative result; declaring a set smaller than the set actually run is the undeclared-sample defect this whole file exists to prevent."
    },
    {
      "id": "RSC-PEERS-2",
      "ask": "What is the generator's peer set, by name, for a suite that must declare it as data?",
      "owner": "entity-system-conformance",
      "state": "NOT ENUMERATED by this session and therefore DECLARED ABSENT rather than guessed. `entity-system-generator`'s `tools/host-launch CLIENT=` slot is named in AGENTS.md as one of the two standardized ways to reach the cohort, and this file should carry a `generator` source. Writing peer names this session did not verify would put an unresolvable name in the one artifact whose job is to make the input auditable.",
      "not_done": "no `generator` source block was invented"
